{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "Some typical variables used in the last 10 years of studies to analyze energy use in hotels include the year of construction, gross floor area (GFA), occupancy rate, number of guestnights, number of guestrooms and star rating [2, 10, 21, 22]. Regarding Energy Use Intensity indicators (EUI), most of the papers reported energy use intensity based on GFA [3, 18], while some reported it based on guest-nights [1, 10, 23] and the number of guestrooms [24]. These contributors to energy use are related to physical building characteristics, and less or no attention was given to those contributors related to economic, environmental, or social outcomes.",
      "reason": "The manuscript describes the current state of knowledge regarding variables used to analyze energy use in hotels and identifies the uncertainty/gap that less attention has been given to economic, environmental, or social outcomes."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "The research fulfils the lack of systematic reviews and meta-analyses considering energy use and its contributors in hotel buildings and the lack of theoretical frameworks on these relationships. The identification of robust empirical findings in the meta-analyses along with the subgroup analysis and meta-analytic regression, understanding the covariates affecting the variables outcomes, can serve as a basis for further research on energy efficiency in hotels.",
      "reason": "The authors explain the importance and need for the review by highlighting the lack of existing systematic reviews and meta-analyses on this topic and the potential for their findings to serve as a basis for further research."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_6",
      "quote": "A search for previous reviews was done, but neither a systematic review nor a meta-analysis involving energy use and its contributors in hotels was found until the day before the protocol registration, October 26, 2023.",
      "reason": "The authors explicitly state that they searched for previous reviews and found none, thereby justifying the need for the current review."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "This is not a review of intervention effects; it is a review of contributors to energy use in hotel buildings."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "This is not a review of complex interventions or delivery contexts; it is a review of contributors to energy use in hotel buildings."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 100
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_4",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "This systematic review and meta-analysis aim to identify contributors to energy use in hotel buildings, provide a descriptive picture of the research stream, and synthesize the theoretical foundation in each variable, and determine the magnitude of the mean effect size on hotels' energy use.",
      "reason": "The manuscript explicitly states the objectives of the review in the introduction section."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e2",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "This is not a review of intervention effects (it is a review of contributors to energy use in hotel buildings), so the requirement to state planned comparisons using PICO does not apply."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 100
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_5",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_6",
      "quote": "To be included, the studies must use quantitative data and investigate the relationship between at least one independent variable (floor area, occupancy rate, number of rooms, carbon intensity, water use, among others) and the energy use in hotel buildings. These variables were always related to hotels or hotel chains of all levels (star rating), open or closed companies. The included studies have assessed the relationships among the variables using univariate or multivariate statistics techniques (i.e., correlations and/or regressions).",
      "reason": "The manuscript specifies the study characteristics for inclusion, including the requirement for quantitative data, the specific relationship to be investigated (independent variables vs. energy use in hotels), the setting (hotels/hotel chains), and the study design (quantitative analysis using correlations/regressions)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_6",
      "quote": "In the report characteristics, there were no restrictions about the publication year or data collection. Conference papers, short communications, dissertations, thesis, or technical reports also be considered due to the possibility of reducing publication bias. ... Any publication language should be considered, but the string keywords will be searched in English.",
      "reason": "The manuscript specifies report characteristics, including publication year (no restrictions), publication status (includes conference papers, dissertations, etc.), and language (any language considered, though search strings were in English)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e3",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The manuscript does not explicitly distinguish between exclusion because outcomes were not measured versus exclusion because outcome results were not reported."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "The subgroup analysis or the meta-regression were done only if the number of studies was at least equal to ten, because investigating two or more factors in a few studies does not provide enough information to look for subgroup interactions [61].",
      "reason": "The manuscript specifies how studies were grouped for synthesis (subgroup analysis and meta-regression) and the criteria for these groupings (e.g., minimum of 10 studies)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e5",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_6",
      "quote": "When papers used the same sample as another study, the more representative (precise) one was maintained in the analysis as the dataset, and the other(s) were used if they presented different variables from the first study. When the studies used the same data and variables, without any difference in the correlations among them, the first published was used in metaanalysis. If the same study presents more than two correlation coefficients in nearby geographic areas, e.g., the same country, then only the two more representative samples were incorporated in the meta-analysis.",
      "reason": "The manuscript provides rationales for restrictions on study eligibility, specifically regarding how to handle overlapping samples or duplicate data to avoid bias."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 100
}

